WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.272
Welcome to Module 4, Aggregating Data with PostgreSQL.

00:00:04.272 --> 00:00:08.792
In the previous module, we worked mainly with individual rows.

00:00:08.792 --> 00:00:14.447
Here, we move from detail records to summaries that can answer analytical questions.

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:21.167
The important idea is that aggregation is not only about writing COUNT, SUM, or AVG.

00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:26.502
Before you calculate anything, decide what one result row should represent.

00:00:26.502 --> 00:00:29.192
That is the grain of the summary.

00:00:29.192 --> 00:00:34.627
Throughout this module, you can run the PostgreSQL examples directly in the browser.

00:00:34.627 --> 00:00:40.982
On code slides, you can also use Copy and paste the same query into the pgAdmin Query Tool

00:00:40.982 --> 00:00:43.017
for additional practice.

00:00:43.017 --> 00:00:48.517
We will build from simple aggregate functions to GROUP BY, WHERE and HAVING, conditional aggregation,

00:00:48.517 --> 00:00:55.402
subtotals, and window functions. Keep asking the same question: what does one row in my result mean?

00:00:55.402 --> 00:01:01.507
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:01:01.507 --> 00:01:06.422
a dashboard summarizing admissions by department and month.

00:01:06.422 --> 00:01:11.762
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:01:11.762 --> 00:01:15.292
analyst can understand our work.

00:01:15.292 --> 00:01:22.167
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:25.877
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:30.060
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
